-- Passage rg6-character-setting-plot-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('60da9a5f-c6c9-5743-973c-c250c0a20ca2', 'rg6-character-setting-plot-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Long Game$q$, $q$Amara Okafor had won every chess game at the Riverside Community Center club since September. She played fast, slapping the clock the moment she moved. Her friends called her "Lightning."

The city youth tournament was different. It was held in the quiet gym of Westbrook Middle School, and each player had a full hour on the clock. In the first round, Amara faced a tall boy named Henrik Solberg, who sat perfectly still and studied the board for minutes at a time.

Amara moved quickly, as usual. Henrik took his time. By the twentieth move, Amara had lost a knight, then a rook. She had walked into a trap she would have seen if she had looked longer. She lost the game in under thirty minutes, with most of her clock still unused.

During the lunch break, she sat alone on the bleachers. Her coach, Ms. Delgado, sat down beside her. "You had fifty minutes left," she said gently. "Time is a piece too. You just didn't use it."

In the second round, Amara made herself a rule. Before each move, she would count to twenty and look at every one of her opponent's pieces. It felt painfully slow. Her leg bounced under the table. But twice she spotted a threat she would have missed, and she won the game.

She lost again in the fourth round, but this time it was a long, close battle. When the tournament ended, Amara had finished ninth out of forty players. On the bus home, she didn't talk about her losses. She told Ms. Delgado she wanted to practice with a longer clock at club.$q$, 'draft')
