-- Passage rg6-character-setting-plot-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0dc46a03-26a8-5261-8bb1-e61ef12493e5', 'rg6-character-setting-plot-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Episode Twelve$q$, $q$Jonah Abiodun and Mei Takahashi produced The Hallway Hour, a weekly podcast for Fairmont Middle School. Every Friday morning, the school played their newest episode during homeroom. Episode twelve was their best yet: an interview with the cafeteria manager about how she feeds six hundred students in forty minutes.

On Thursday night, Jonah opened their shared folder to make one last edit. The audio file was gone. He searched the trash, the downloads, and every other folder. Nothing. Somehow, the file had been deleted.

He texted Mei in a panic. "It's gone. All of it. We have to cancel."

Mei called him back. She was calmer than he was, but only a little. "We can't record the interview again tonight," she said. "But we still have our notes."

Jonah wanted to argue. Without the interview, there was no episode. Then he looked at his notebook and saw the manager's best answers, copied down word for word.

"What if we tell the story ourselves?" he said. "We explain what she told us, read her best lines, and then admit what happened to the file. People might like hearing that part."

They recorded at Mei's kitchen table, with a towel draped over the microphone to soften the hum of the refrigerator. It took three tries. The episode was shorter than usual and a little rough.

On Friday, students in Room 14 laughed at the part about the lost file. At lunch, the cafeteria manager stopped by their table and offered to do a second interview the next week. That afternoon, Jonah set up a backup drive and saved every file twice.$q$, 'draft')
